<speak>
<p>Suppose T of X has odd integer coefficients on a cycle of length two to the k. Its product with T star is two to the power two t, times w squared, where t is at least two and w is odd. We will halve the cycle, keep every coefficient odd, and divide the scalar norm by four. The parameters will change from k, t to k minus one, t minus one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The comparison lemma says that opposite coefficients agree modulo two to the t. Since t is at least two, they agree modulo four. Write one opposite pair as a sub j and a sub j plus four r sub j. Their average is a sub j plus two r sub j: an odd integer plus an even integer, hence still odd. For example, three and seven average to five.<break time="700ms"/>Apply this averaging to every opposite pair. The old cycle folds onto a cycle of half the length, with one odd average at each new position.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now check the norm. Let q reduce exponents modulo the half length, and call the polynomial of averages T one. Opposite coefficients add under projection, so q of T equals twice T one. The projection preserves multiplication and star. Applying it to the original norm identity therefore gives four times T one times T one star equals two to the power two t, times w squared.<break time="700ms"/>Divide the integer coefficients by four. This is valid because the integer group ring has a free coefficient basis. The new norm is two raised to twice the difference between t and one, times w squared. One fold has halved the length and divided the norm by four.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Keep the indices visible as we repeat. Start with k equal to two s and t equal to s, assuming s is at least two. After j folds, k equals two s minus j, and t equals s minus j. The initial row, j equal to zero, is the projection from Node two point three. Whenever j is less than s minus one, t is still at least two, so the next fold is allowed and every coefficient stays odd.<break time="700ms"/>After exactly s minus one folds, k equals s plus one and t equals one. The length is two to the power s plus one, and the norm is four w squared. Stop here. We do not apply another oddness preserving fold at t equal to one. The next node uses this endpoint to obtain the contradiction.<break time="1100ms"/></p>
</speak>
